const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What does the MC5R receptor do?',
    a: 'MC5R’s best-defined job is controlling exocrine glands — most clearly the sebaceous glands that produce the sebum and lipids that oil skin and fur. It also appears on lacrimal and other exocrine tissues and on immune cells, where its role is still being mapped.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do we know MC5R controls sebum?',
    a: 'When the Mc5r gene is deleted in mice, the animals develop defective water repulsion and thermoregulation traced to reduced sebaceous lipid production, along with impairment of other exocrine glands — showing that one receptor coordinates secretion across several gland types.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is MC5R involved in acne?',
    a: 'Possibly. Because MC5R helps regulate sebaceous output, it is of interest in sebum-driven skin conditions such as acne, and modulating it is one theoretical route to reducing sebum. This is an area of active research rather than established treatment.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is MC5R the least understood melanocortin receptor?',
    a: 'Its messenger RNA appears in a very wide range of tissues, which long obscured a single core function, and its human physiology is still being mapped. The clearest role — exocrine secretion — comes largely from mouse knockouts; the immune and metabolic roles are less settled.',
  },
];

const references = [
  {
